  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nCurrently, scattered thunderstorms remain just north of the \nInternational Border, in northwest and north central ND, but do\nextend south as you get to the Turtle Mountains area. Elsewhere,\nIsolated showers and thunderstorms were located over eastern\nMontana.  \n\nFor this afternoon and tonight, there is some elevated\ninstability across northern and western portions of the \nforecast area. There is a marginal risk (level 1 out of 5) of \nsevere storms over the far southwest (mainly Bowman and Adams \nCounties). Surface low pressure over southeast Montana is \nforecast to move into southwest ND later this afternoon. \nSouthwest ND will be in an area of surface convergence ahead of \nthe low. There is on the order of 500-1000 J/kg of elevated \nCAPE above an area of good CIN. The far southwest looks to be \nthe one area in the west which could see most of the CIN erode \nlater this afternoon into early evening. Every so often one of \nthe CAMs will show a few stronger cells over the far southwest. \nA combination of the decreased CIN and surface convergence could\nsupport a strong to severe storms with the main threat being \nwind gusts to 60 mph. In the north convection continues along \nthe International Border. Instability does increase with good \nshear as you move east across the north central. The SPC \nmesoanalysis page indicated increasing CIN over both western and\nnorthern ND later this afternoon into this evening. Therefore, \nthe overall threat for severe storms looks to remain low.\n\nTonight CAMs do show scattered shower/thunderstorm activity \nspreading west to east over the southwest and south central, \nalong with scattered convection along the north spreading south \nand east. Really uncertain about overall coverage of any \nactivity tonight given the general strong capping, so stuck more\nto a broadbrush of slight chance pops through tonight. \n\nElevated smoke looks to remain over the area for the next few \ndays, so kept a mention of smoke in the forecast and put a lower\nlimit on the sky cover through Tuesday.\n\nTemperatures climb higher Tuesday and Wednesday. Tuesday looks\nto be mostly dry with upper level ridging building over the\narea. The lack of low level moisture should also keep heat index\nvalues below critical values, even as surface temperatures climb\ninto the upper 90s south and west of the Missouri River.\nOn Wednesday, temperatures are about the same as or a little \nhigher than Tuesday. A southerly return flow should also bump up\ndew point temperatures a little. This could bump apparent \ntemperatures high enough for a Heat Advisory. However, the \nuncertainty is high enough at this time to give it another day. \nCurrently it appears that it would probably be a limited area \nand for a limited duration so would most likely pass if we were \nneeding to make a decision today. \n\nWednesday night and Thursday we see a series of waves\npropagating through the mean upper ridge, bringing us better\nchances for showers and thunderstorms than we have seen in a\nwhile. Right now things look a bit messy with perhaps a southern\nstream and a northern stream wave traversing the forecast area\nwith a lot of uncertainty in timing and placement of said waves\nand resulting convection. NBM continues with it's broadbrush\narea of medium to high pops Wednesday night and Thursday. As was\nmentioned yesterday, not overly excited about the areal extent\nof high pops but this far out. a broadbrush approach seems \njustified. \n\nTemperatures do drop a little after Wednesday, with highs mainly\nin the mid 80s to mid 90s Thursday and Friday, followed by a\nwarm-up as we head into the weekend.",
